    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a potent offensive profile, anchored by **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (82.13), who headlines our lineup. Batting lead-off against a favorable matchup with McGreevy, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** benefits from a massive 10% boost for his high-leverage order spot and a +14% dynamic platoon advantage, making him our premier target for stolen base production today. Following closely are **Yordan Alvarez** (70.43) and **Lars Nootbaar** (67.77). **Yordan Alvarez** remains a locked-in core piece despite a difficult matchup, as his elite 0.464 xwOBA renders traditional SP skill concerns secondary. Meanwhile, **Lars Nootbaar** thrives in his lead-off role against Holmes, leveraging a +16% SP skill advantage and +11% platoon edge to provide essential table-setting production.\n\nStrategic roster management today centered on maximizing ceiling against mid-tier arms while navigating the risk of top-tier pitching. The most difficult decision involved **Christian Encarnacion-Strand** (43.86), who narrowly missed the starting cut. While he offers a solid 0.385 xwOBA, his -16% SP skill penalty against Seymour creates a basement that renders him less efficient than options like **Chase Meidroth** (48.42) or **Caleb Durbin** (48.56). We have flagged **Kazuma Okamoto** as our primary risk, as his start is necessitated by roster depth rather than matchup favorability; he faces Schlittler, an elite arm with a 2.81 xERA, resulting in a -19% SP skill penalty that drags his efficiency down to a marginal 42.45. Managers should monitor weather updates for the **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber** games; while both carry strong scores (62.60 and 65.94), they face Prielipp, and any weather-related delays could impact our total game cap efficiency. **Dillon Dingler** (63.75) rounds out the high-efficiency tier, with his +14% platoon edge against Kay serving as a vital driver for our catcher production today.",
